For this project, I'd stick with OpenAI as primary, with Ollama as your offline fallback — which is actually already what your README hints at.

The reasoning: your `LlmJudgeScorer` needs a model that reasons well enough to give consistent hallucination/accuracy grades — local models (via Ollama) are noticeably weaker judges and can introduce noise into the very benchmark scores you're trying to showcase. OpenAI's models are more reliable for both the answering and judging roles, which matters for a demo about *measuring* accuracy.

The tradeoff is live-demo risk: OpenAI means depending on network + API key mid-presentation. Since your README already calls out pre-seeding the graph the night before and recording a backup run, I'd apply that same instinct here — keep OpenAI for quality, but consider having an Ollama-backed fallback path wired in (or at least tested) in case conference wifi fails.

